Here's the explanation of the parent function $f(x) = |x|$ and its transformation $g(x) = 2|x+4| - 3$:

### Visual
```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2, transform shape]
    % Axes
    \draw[->, thick] (-6, 0) -- (2, 0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->, thick] (0, -4) -- (0, 4) node[above] {$y$};
    \foreach \x in {-6, -5, -4, -3, -2, -1, 1, 2}
        \draw (\x, 0.1) -- (\x, -0.1) node[below] {$\x$};
    \foreach \y in {-4, -3, -2, -1, 1, 2, 3, 4}
        \draw (0.1, \y) -- (-0.1, \y) node[left] {$\y$};

    % Grid
    \draw[gray!30, dashed] (-6, -4) grid (2, 4);

    % Parent function
    \draw[gray, thick, domain=-6:2, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {abs(\x)});

    % Transformed function
    \draw[blue, very thick, domain=-6:2, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {2*abs(\x+4)-3});

    % New vertex
    \filldraw[black] (-4, -3) circle (2pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
```

### Explanation
- The parent function $f(x) = |x|$ is a [h]V-shaped absolute value function[/h].
- The transformed function $g(x) = 2|x+4| - 3$ has the following transformations:
  - [h]Horizontal shift[/h] left by 4 units
  - [h]Vertical stretch[/h] by a factor of 2
  - [h]Vertical shift[/h] down by 3 units
- The new vertex is at [u]$(h, k) = (-4, -3)$[/u], which is [c]circled[/c] on the graph.
- The [b]Range[/b] of the transformed function is $[y \geq -3]$.
- Note that there is [s]no vertical compression[/s] in this transformation.

This transformation is a fundamental example in the [b]Functions[/b] domain of Algebra 2, specifically under standard [b]A2.F.2[/b], which focuses on analyzing transformations of parent functions.

