Here's the TikZ code for an acute triangle ABC with its inscribed circle, following all the specified constraints:

```tikz
\documentclass[tikz,border=3.14mm]{standalone}
\usetikzlibrary{calc, intersections, angles, quotes}

\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5, transform shape]
    % Define triangle vertices
    \coordinate (A) at (0,4);
    \coordinate (B) at (-3,0);
    \coordinate (C) at (5,0);

    % Draw triangle
    \draw[thick] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;

    % Label vertices
    \node[anchor=south] at (A) {A};
    \node[anchor=north] at (B) {B};
    \node[anchor=north] at (C) {C};

    % Find angle bisector of A
    \path[name path=bisectorA] (A) -- ($(B)!(A)!(C)$);

    % Find angle bisector of B
    \path[name path=bisectorB] (B) -- ($(A)!(B)!(C)$);

    % Find incenter
    \path[name intersections={of=bisectorA and bisectorB, by=I}];

    % Find radius by projecting I onto AB
    \coordinate (P) at ($(A)!(I)!(B)$);

    % Draw incircle
    \draw[thick, blue] (I) let \p1 = ($(I) - (P)$) in circle ({veclen(\x1,\y1)});

    % Draw angle bisectors
    \draw[dashed] (A) -- (I);
    \draw[dashed] (B) -- (I);

    % Mark incenter
    \filldraw[black] (I) circle (1pt) node[above right] {I};

    % Find points of tangency
    \coordinate (D) at ($(A)!(I)!(C)$);
    \coordinate (E) at ($(B)!(I)!(C)$);
    \coordinate (F) at ($(A)!(I)!(B)$);

    % Draw perpendicular lines from I to sides
    \draw[dashed] (I) -- (D);
    \draw[dashed] (I) -- (E);
    \draw[dashed] (I) -- (F);

    % Mark points of tangency
    \filldraw[black] (D) circle (1pt);
    \filldraw[black] (E) circle (1pt);
    \filldraw[black] (F) circle (1pt);

    % Add grid for reference
    \draw[gray!30, dashed] (-4,-1) grid (6,5);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```

This code:
1. Uses only standard TikZ libraries (calc, intersections, angles, quotes)
2. Defines the triangle vertices at A(0,4), B(-3,0), and C(5,0)
3. Constructs the incenter using angle bisectors of A and B
4. Finds the intersection of these bisectors to locate the incenter (I)
5. Calculates the radius by projecting I onto AB and using the distance formula
6. Draws the incircle using the calculated radius
7. Labels all points with appropriate anchors to push labels outward
8. Includes dashed lines showing the angle bisectors and perpendiculars to the sides
9. Follows all the specified constraints without using tkz-euclide

The code ensures that the incenter and incircle are constructed mathematically without any hardcoded values, and all labels are positioned to avoid overlapping with other elements.

